  "theVerdictDivergenceThisExplains": {
    "what": "VF15 failed four Washington families on exactly these four obligations. VF16 passed five siblings off the same host with the same absence, because it scored the nine completeness counters and not the fifteen proof obligations.",
    "settled": "Captain downgraded the five on obligations measured directly, and lane contract L9 now refuses a PASS_COMPLETE_INDEPENDENT from a return that scored a subset. Nine of the seventeen in this wave are those Washington families."
  },
  "whatARepairerMustNotDo": [
    "Do not write a court name, a filing fee, a waiver route or a service rule that is not sourced. An unsourced filing instruction is worse than an absent one: the participant acts on it.",
    "Do not copy another state's instructions. The four obligations are jurisdiction-specific and a plausible-looking wrong court is the most damaging possible output.",
    "Do not touch the packet PDFs. This is a missing document beside them, not a defect in them, and nine of these families hold a hash-bound raster receipt that a byte change would invalidate.",
    "Where the fee, the court or the service rule is genuinely unsourced, return that obligation BLOCKED_SOURCE or BLOCKED_LEGAL_INPUT and say which. Six of these families are not even queued for raster and may be source-blocked already."
  ],
  "theHouseStandard": "VT delegates the fee to a named authority rather than stating a number, and VF-SRC-A treated that as passing. Delegation to a named, checkable authority is acceptable; a seven-item catch-all naming 'fees' is not, which is why ri_nonconviction_sealing-set fails FEE_AND_WAIVER with all nine counters at zero.",
  "ceiling": "Writing instructions promotes nothing. Each family still needs its raster receipt and an independent read by a worker that did not write them.",
